  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They held what they built until someone took it, then took it back in the eighth.",
    "what_this_means": "Lopez delivered six innings of one-run baseball. Precision under control. And that should have been enough. But the offense couldn't find anything until the late innings aligned: a chance showed up, Heim converted it with contact, and suddenly the lead flipped again. This is the formula when it works: the starter holds the door open long enough for someone to walk through it late. Medina leaked two runs in the seventh and nearly cost them, but Alvarado closed it clean. They are not supposed to win games like this on the road. Uneven, fragile, with the margin erased and reclaimed in one inning. That they did suggests something is taking shape, even if it doesn't look like much yet. The bullpen held when it mattered. The bats arrived just before it was too late.",
    "what_to_watch": "Cristian Javier comes in with a 6.44 ERA tonight. The kind of pitcher this lineup should find something against if it stays awake long enough to look.",
